# 113. Bottom View of Binary Tree

## APPROACH: BOTTOM VIEW OF BINARY TREE (BFS / LEVEL ORDER + VERTICAL OVERWRITE)

Idea:
Neeche se dekhne par har vertical column (`col`) me sirf wahi node visible hogi jo sabse aakhiri (bottom-most level par) aati hai.
Horizontal distance coordinates:
- Root starts at `col = 0`.
- Left child goes to `col - 1`.
- Right child goes to `col + 1`.
BFS (Level Order Traversal) upar se neeche levels visit karta hai.
Har baar jab hum kisi column ki node visit karte hain, map me us column ki value ko **blindly overwrite** kar dete hain (`map.put(currCol, currNode.data)`).
Loop khatam hone tak har column me automatically sabse last level par aane wali node hi bachegi.

--------------------------------------------------

APPROACH (BFS using Queue + TreeMap)

1. Edge Case:
   Agar `root == null` ho, empty list return kar do.

2. Data Structures:
   - Helper class `Pair`: `(node, col)` track karne ke liye.
   - `Queue<Pair> q`: Level order traversal ke liye.
   - `Map<Integer, Integer> map = new TreeMap<>()`:
     Key = `col` (Left to right sorted automatically), Value = `node.data`.

3. BFS Traversal:
   - Queue me root push karo: `q.offer(new Pair(root, 0))`.
   - Jab tak queue empty na ho:
     - Element pop karo: `currNode` aur uska `currCol`.
     - **Core Condition (Bottom View Trick):**
       Pehle se value present ho ya na ho, directly overwrite karo:
       `map.put(currCol, currNode.data);`
       *(Jo node baad me aayegi wahi pichli node ko bottom se hide kar degi).*
     - Left child push karo with `currCol - 1`.
     - Right child push karo with `currCol + 1`.

4. Result Extraction:
   `TreeMap` keys automatically sorted (-2, -1, 0, 1, 2...) order me rehti hain.
   `map.values()` ko list me daal kar return kar do.

--------------------------------------------------

EXAMPLE

Tree:
          10 (col 0)
        /    \
    20 (-1)   30 (+1)
   /    \
40 (-2)  60 (0)

Dry Run:
- Process 10 (col 0): map[0] = 10
- Process 20 (col -1): map[-1] = 20
- Process 30 (col 1): map[1] = 30
- Process 40 (col -2): map[-2] = 40
- Process 60 (col 0): map[0] = 60  <- 10 overwrite hoke 60 ban gaya!

Sorted Map Entries:
-2 -> 40
-1 -> 20
 0 -> 60
 1 -> 30

Result: [40, 20, 60, 30]

--------------------------------------------------

WHY BFS AND NOT DFS?

- **Same-level overlap rule:**
  Agar do nodes same level aur same horizontal distance par hain, toh problem statement kehta hai:
  *"consider the node that appears later in level traversal."*
- BFS left-to-right aur level-by-level execute karta hai, isliye later node naturally pehle aane wali node ko queue processing ke dauran cleanly overwrite kar deti hai bina kisi extra level tracking logic ke.

--------------------------------------------------

COMPLEXITY

N = Total number of nodes in tree

Time:
O(N log N) -> Har node queue me ek baar aati hai, aur `TreeMap` me update $O(\log K)$ leta hai ($K \le N$).
*(Agar minCol aur maxCol manually track karke normal `HashMap` use karein toh time O(N) ho sakta hai).*

Space:
O(N) -> Queue aur TreeMap dono me at most $N$ nodes store hote hain.
*(Excluding output list ans)*

--------------------------------------------------

INTERVIEW POINT

- Top View vs Bottom View (Ek Line ka difference):
  - **Top View:** `if (!map.containsKey(col)) map.put(col, val);` (Only first encounter).
  - **Bottom View:** `map.put(col, val);` (Always overwrite on every encounter).
- Overlap Rule:
  Interview me clarifying question zaroor poocho: "Agar do nodes same column aur same row par hain, toh kaunsi node pick karni hai?" Standard BFS right child ko queue me baad me daalta hai, isliye right/later appearing node safely pick ho jaati hai.

--------------------------------------------------

# CODE:

import java.util.*;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {

    class Pair {
        TreeNode node;
        int col;
        Pair(TreeNode node, int col) {
            this.node = node;
            this.col = col;
        }
    }

    public List<Integer> bottomView(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return ans;

        Queue<Pair> q = new LinkedList<>();
        // TreeMap keeps columns sorted from leftmost to rightmost
        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new TreeMap<>();

        q.offer(new Pair(root, 0));

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            Pair curr = q.poll();
            TreeNode currNode = curr.node;
            int currCol = curr.col;

            // Hamesha overwrite karenge taaki latest/bottom-most node bache
            map.put(currCol, currNode.data);

            if (currNode.left != null) {
                q.offer(new Pair(currNode.left, currCol - 1));
            }
            if (currNode.right != null) {
                q.offer(new Pair(currNode.right, currCol + 1));
            }
        }

        // Sorted columns ke values list me extract karo
        for (int val : map.values()) {
            ans.add(val);
        }

        return ans;
    }
}
